บ dots บ rhythmic time value บ number over บ centering บ horz. displ. บ
ศออออออสอออออออออออออออออออออสอออออออออออออสอออออออออออสออออออออออออออผ

ษออออออออออหออออออออออหออออออหออออออออออหออออออออออออออออหอออออออออออออออป
บ   p11    บ    p12   บ p13  บ   p14    บ      p15       บ     p16       บ
บ num hgt. บ num size บ cues บ num font บ rest sz factor บnum horz offsetบ
ศออออออออออสออออออออออสออออออสออออออออออสออออออออออออออออสอออออออออออออออผ

For mini-rests (used with grace notes) add 100 to P4 (or -100 if P4 is minus.)


*
                                                                          2/7
P5 rest types: 0 = quarter       -1 = half
               1 = eighth        -2 = whole
               2 = sixteenth     -3 = breve (double whole)
               3 = 32nd          -4 = repeat bar sign
               4 = 64th          -5 = long, multiple bar rest  (Must have
                    etc.               number in P8, and must use P9 to
                                       be centered.)
                                 -6 = narrow double whole
                                 -7 = 4-bar rest


       Composite rests for orchestra parts (European style).
         (The appropriate number should be put in P8.)

  P5 = -11 = 1 bar rest     P5 = -14 = 4 bar rest     P5 = -17 = 7 bar rest            P5 = -14 = 4 b
  P5 = -12 = 2 bar rest     P5 = -15 = 5 bar rest     P5 = -18 = 8 bar rest            P5 = -14 = 4 b
  P5 = -13 = 3 bar rest     P5 = -16 = 6 bar rest     P5 = -19 = 9 bar rest            P5 = -14 = 4 b






*
                                                                          3/7
P6 normally holds the number of dots needed.  However, if P6 = -1 the rest
  becomes invisible.  Invisible rests are sometimes needed so the automatic
  alignment system can work properly.  The main input routine (IN) will often
  use invisible rests when there must be more than 1 musical voice on a
  single staff.  If P6 is > 4 a horizontal line will appear above the rest.
  This line may be used for visual centering of rests.  (The line will NOT
  appear in hard copy output.)  The length of this line equals the value of P6.

P7 holds rhythmic time values (always needed for automatic spacing systems).
   For a normal quarter rest P7=1; for an eighth, P7=.5; for a half, P7=2;
   for a whole rest, P7=4 (i.e. 4 times as long as a quarter rest).
   A triplet eighth = .3333, or 1/3 of the time of a quarter.


P8 holds the number to be put over a rest indicating a certain number of
   bars rest to be counted.

P8 = -1 puts a fermata over a rest.  [RF]





*
                                                                          4/7
   
   When P9 is set to -1 the rest will automatically be centered between 
   the position given in P3 and the position of the next bar line found.  
   The program then inserts the actual position value into P9.  If the 
   music is shifted due to justifying or re-aligning you must enter edit 
   mode and reset P9 to -1 to re-center the rest.

CW  --  Typing CW (center whole rest) has the same effect as setting
   P9 to -1.  CW and P9 work only when P5 is < -1.


P10 = horizontal displacement of rest from normal position as set by P3.

P11 = height of attached number or fermata over rest.
                            Use this to change from normal height.

P12 = size of number or fermata over rest.  Default size = 1.






*
                                                                          5/7

P13 may hold information for searching for cues when the part extraction
    feature of the PAGE program is used.  When a part has a whole measure
    rest, P13 can hold the number of the instrument where cue notes are to
    be found.  (The instrument numbers must be given in P9 of each staff.)


P14 not =0 will override the default type font for numbers over rests.
    (The default is set in the PREF.SCR file by the 'restnum' line.)
    Aside from zero, the type font numbers used here follow the same
    rules as those used for font changes in Code-10 (numbers), P7.


P15 is a general rest size factor.  (But remember that the various auto-
  matic spacing and justifying features will NOT take into account rest
  size changes made with P15.)


P16 can move the rest number to left or right.




*
                                                                          6/7

___________________________________________________________________________
------- Letter Commands while editing Rests -------------------------------

>>> Rhythm
 CUE   = make rest cue size. (P4 + 100)
 CUX   = normal size rest.  (P4 < 100)
 RH64  = 64th rest. (P5, 7)
 RH32  = 32nd rest. (P5, 7)
 RHD   = makes double whole rest. (P5, 7).
 RHS   = makes 1/16 rest. (P5, 7).
 RHQ   = makes 1/4 rest. (P5, 7).
 RHH   = makes 1/2 rest. (P5, 7).
 RHW   = makes whole rest. (P5, 7).
 RHG   = makes grace rest. (P4, 7, 8, 9)
 RHU   = change rhythm to quintuplet. (P7)
                    e.g. P7=.5 (1/8) becomes =.4 (1/10)
 TRP   = change rhythm to triplet. (P7)
                    e.g. P7=.5 (1/8) becomes =.333 (1/12)
                TRP and RHU are ignored if original rhythm is not
                a basic duple value. (1/2, 1/4, 1/8, etc.)


*
                                                                          7/7


>>> Dots  (P6)
 DOT   = adds dot to rest - changes P7, 6.
 DDT   = make double dot. (P7, 6)
 SPD   = space dot to right
 SPD-  = space dot to left
 


>>> Offsets
 OFM   = offset minus, moves rest .5 step to left. (P10)
 OFP   = offset plus, moves rest .5 step to right.
 NOF   = no offset of rest - P10 = 0.

 RF    = add fermata.  (P8 = -1)
 RI    = invisible/visible (P6 = -1 or = 0)
 RP    = repeat bar ./.  (P5 = -4)
 WBn   = whole bar rest with number n
 EUn   = European style whole bar composite rest with number n


-------------------------------------------------------------------------end---
